= Rio de Janeiro, February 1962

The founding congress is behind us. The break with the PCB is made, the manifestos printed and distributed through the networks that still hold, the names signed to documents that would mean imprisonment if the wrong hands found them. João Amazonas presides over a party that is, by any honest reckoning, very small. But it is ours, and it is clear-eyed about what is coming in a way the PCB has ceased to be.

Brazil under Goulart is a country in motion. The peasant leagues of Francisco Julião have put the land question — that wound that never closes — at the center of national politics. The CGT holds general strikes that shake São Paulo. The students of the UNE fill the streets with a politics that is looking for somewhere to go. Goulart himself is not a revolutionary — he is a *getulista*, a populist who inherited his politics from Vargas — but the forces gathering around his government are something more than that, or could be, if they found direction.

The right knows this. The ESG-trained officers in the barracks know this. The UDN in Congress knows this. The American embassy on Flamengo beach knows this. They are not going to wait indefinitely. The question is not whether the crisis will come, but when — and whether we will be ready.

We were not ready in April 1964. That is the history we are trying to change.